<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Foggara</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">from c. 500 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A foggara is an underground irrigation tunnel that taps <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundwater-depletion">groundwater</a> and carries it by gravity to the surface, similar to the Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qanat">qanat</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garamantian-empire">Garamantian Empire</a> built an extensive foggara network in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fezzan">Fezzan</a> from c. 500 BCE, enabling agriculture in the desert. Falling groundwater levels eventually made the tunnels fail, contributing to Garamantian decline.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/foggara.jpg" alt="Foggara" width="300" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tavasoli mohsen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ghasabe_Qanats_of_Gonabad2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
